course: dictionary
lesson: 14095
title: Memorable words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Something memorable is worth remembering, so you will still think about it long after. Memorable is an adjective, which means it describes a noun, like a day or a moment. You can say «a memorable day», «a memorable moment» or «make it memorable».
explain: Memorable can mean special, unusual, very good or even very bad. «Our trip to the snowy mountains was memorable.» The word does not say the trip was nice. It says the trip is one you will remember.
explain: Memorable can also mean easy to remember. «The song has a catchy, memorable tune.» The tune is so simple that it sticks in your head. When you meet memorable, ask yourself: is this special, or is it easy to remember?
explain: Writers in English lessons try to make story openings memorable, so the reader wants to keep reading. Adverts are memorable when a slogan sticks in your mind. Birthdays, holidays and school trips can be memorable too.
fact: Memorable means worth remembering.
fact: «Memorable» can mean special, unusual, very good or very bad.
fact: «Memorable» can also mean easy to remember, like a short phrase or a tune.
fact: Memorable is an adjective: more memorable, most memorable.
exercise: «The day at the seaside was memorable.» What does memorable mean here? | Boring and quick to forget | *Worth remembering | Very cold | Sad and lost
exercise: «The catchy song had a memorable tune.» What does memorable suggest about the tune? | Very long and slow | Hard to sing | Written in a different language | *Easy to remember
exercise: «My birthday party was so ___ that I still talk about it.» Which word fits the gap? | *memorable | quiet | boring | empty
test: «The teacher said our class trip would be memorable.» What does memorable mean? | Too short to notice | Dull and tiring | *Worth remembering | Forgotten by everyone
test: Which phrase means a special moment you will remember? | *A memorable moment | A rushed moment | An empty moment | A forgettable moment
exam: Ruby says «memorable» only means very good things. Is she right? | Yes, but only in adverts | No, it means quick and cheap | Yes, only very good things are memorable | *No, it can be very bad as well as very good
